#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:4.4.t94-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:4.4.t118
REASONING=Two verbal expressions: "celebrabantur" (independent, transitive passive — 'eius adventus' nom. pl. as subject) and "superaret" (dependent result clause introduced by "ut", transitive active). "post" here is the adverb ('afterwards'), not a preposition (it is immediately followed by the preposition "in" governing the ablative "ceteris ... partibus"), so it is adverbial to "celebrabantur". "in" is adverbial to "celebrabantur"; "partibus" and "Graecia" are both objects of "in", joined by the enclitic "-que" (t100), which therefore relates to "partibus" and "Graecia". "ceteris" is adjectival to "partibus", "cuncta" adjectival to "Graecia", "Asiae" genitive with "partibus". "sic" is adverbial to "celebrabantur" (anticipating the ut-clause), and "eius" is genitive with "adventus" (t104). In the ut-clause, one verb "superaret" serves both limbs (zeugma): first limb — "exspectatio" (with genitive "hominis") subject, "famam" (with genitive "ingeni") direct object; second limb — "adventus" (nom. sg., with genitive "ipsius") and "admiratio" subjects joined by the enclitic "-que" (t116), "exspectationem" direct object. A possible alternative reading takes "ipsius adventus" as a genitive phrase depending on "admiratio" ('admiration of his very arrival'), leaving "admiratio" the sole subject; I chose the reading in which "adventus" is a nominative subject coordinated with "admiratio" by "-que", since the -que then has two parallel nominatives to join and the sense ('his arrival and the admiration for it surpassed expectation') matches the escalating series famam → exspectationem. This is a genuine ambiguity in the case of "adventus" (t114).

